Your phone isn’t actually “thinking it was in Japan.” The warning is about the keyboard app itself: Android is telling you that an input method can see everything you type, including passwords and credit card numbers, because that’s how keyboards work.

So the meaning is:

- if you enable that keyboard, it could potentially read and collect whatever you type;
- that includes sensitive text like passwords, messages, and payment details;
- Android shows the warning to remind you of that risk before you trust the app.

It doesn’t mean the keyboard will necessarily steal anything, just that it has the capability to see all your input once enabled.

No. This permission does not let an app freely read your emails, calendar, messages, documents, or similar data.

What it does cover is access through Android’s account system. An app with this permission can ask AccountManager to use an account that’s already on the device, for example to get an authentication token so it can sign in to an online service on your behalf. The system may prompt you to approve that access.

So the closest supported answer is:

- it can request to use account credentials already present on the device;
- it can then act on your behalf for the specific service/account it is authorized for;
- it does not automatically grant broad access to all your personal data on the device.

